---
title: "Lamport's mutex over FIFO channels: B waits its turn"
---
sequenceDiagram
    participant L0 as A
    participant L1 as B
    Note over L0: request<br/>L     1<br/>queue (1,A)<br/>heard B:0<br/>holds no
    Note over L1: request<br/>L     1<br/>queue (1,B)<br/>heard A:0<br/>holds no
    L1->>L0: req 1
    Note over L0: req from B<br/>L     3<br/>queue (1,A) (1,B)<br/>heard B:1<br/>holds yes
    L0->>L1: req 1
    Note over L1: req from A<br/>L     3<br/>queue (1,A) (1,B)<br/>heard A:1<br/>holds no
    Note over L0,L1: A's request (1,A) reaches B before anything A sent after it, and goes ahead of B's own (1,B) in B's queue
    L0->>L1: ack 3
    Note over L1: ack from A<br/>L     4<br/>queue (1,A) (1,B)<br/>heard A:3<br/>holds no
    Note over L0,L1: B has heard from A at 3, later than its request, but A's request is first in its queue, so B waits
    L1->>L0: ack 3
    Note over L0: ack from B<br/>L     4<br/>queue (1,A) (1,B)<br/>heard B:3<br/>holds yes
    Note over L0: release<br/>L     5<br/>queue (1,B)<br/>heard B:3<br/>holds no
    L0->>L1: release 5
    Note over L1: release from A<br/>L     6<br/>queue (1,B)<br/>heard A:5<br/>holds yes
    Note over L0,L1: A releases: B drops (1,A) from its queue, its own request is first, and B enters
